From 2dc9c49f5caac336f475c02730e65a489c0f2e89 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stew Forster <ci@rdna-boosts.invalid>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 10:35:05 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] wip(host-read-transport): the adaptive host read transport +
 `--load-mode buffered`

TODO #306 widened at the maintainer's direction: the question is not the pool FILL's source but the TRANSPORT of
every per-expert host read.  At r261 the coalesced prefill span reads the FILE in both modes (r232) while the pool
fill and the demand/bounce read take the file arm ONLY under MOE_HOST_POOL_DIO=1, so a mapped load serves both out
of the model mapping -- one demand fault per 4 KiB page, measured collapsing to 593 MiB/s under a 1 GiB cache
budget and to 1 537-1 628 MiB/s fills / 5.5-6.3 t/s decode in a starving 32G scope.

WHAT THIS ADDS

  * `--load-mode buffered` (new): a MAPPED master whose host reads are buffered `pread`s -- the combination that
    was unrepresentable because r212 welded the fill transport to `--load-mode`.  Explicit `mmap` / `buffered` /
    `dio` LOCK the transport; `auto` (the default) is the adaptive one; `none` / `mlock` / `mmap+mlock` keep
    today's meaning.  Touches include/llama.h, common/arg.cpp, src/llama.cpp (to/from str), the loader's use_mmap
    and its transport hook, llama-bench's mode list.
  * ADAPTIVE `auto`: starts optimistically on the mapping and falls down `map -> file -> dio` on two DIMENSIONLESS
    triggers -- a stall (K x this arm's own running mean, N times running) and a probe-fill A/B (every Nth fill
    is served by the next arm; same bytes, so no extra I/O, and two latencies in the same cache state at the same
    moment).  `dio` needs 3x or a stall, because it bypasses the page cache and a single-read win can be a mirage.
  * ONE WAY BY DECISION: a box that fell short once is likely to again, and the page cache a reverse switch would
    rebuild may itself be the cause, so a slower consistent point beats a flapping optimum.
  * cgroup-aware `pool_available_ram_bytes()` (min of MemAvailable and this process's own cgroup) -- the
    root-cause fix for the pinned-pool RAM ceiling, which had the same host-wide bug.
  * the span coupling ENFORCED, not warned: with MOE_HOST_POOL_STAGE_SPAN=0 the buffered arm is refused and the
    ladder steps over `file` to `dio`, so the measured -41 % pair is unreachable.

EVIDENCE (all in wip/host-read-transport/)

  * gate 24/24: 7 text arms (no -lv, hashed) + 7 census arms (-lv 4), every arm proving its own transport via
    its own counters, the ladder proven to move under a starving cgroup, the coupling proven refused, and `auto`
    graded on decode throughput against the best pinned arm.
  * text identical on every arm: 144 chars sha=de81b28cab7d.
  * interleaved ABBA (cache dropped per arm): unpressured 44G -- map/file/auto all 25.9-26.2 t/s decode, dio 11.2;
    starving 32G+ballast -- map 5.5-6.3, file 12.8-12.9, dio 11.3, and `auto` reached `file` on the STALL
    detector at 6.5 s ("4 consecutive reads over 4.0x this arm's own 498 us").
  * batched-bench npl 1/4/8 (35B-A3B, cacheable): map 63.72/186.52/276.84, file 61.30/183.93/276.72, auto stays
    map at 62.21/185.24/276.00 -- no regression at width.

BUG FOUND BY THE MATRIX (fixed): the probe's cadence counter was incremented only by the observe path a probe
returns BEFORE, so once a probe succeeded every fill became a probe (42 746 probes serving 42 746 fills; after
the ladder reached `file` every probe was a `dio` read -- 67 ms/token against 39).  The cadence is now counted on
the wrapper line itself: 1 in 128, 15-66 ms per run, and `auto` is neutral when it does not latch.

OWED BEFORE PROMOTION: the probe thresholds are reasoned, not tuned (the one-off runs that tried to set them were
inconclusive because the box drifted -- the same pinned arm read 12.8 t/s in the matrix and 11.3 ten minutes
later); the MTP four-axis protocol has not been run (host-side change, no kernel or numerics, but it is the
contract for a decode-path change); and no Windows-form measurement exists.
